('127.462.d.6.B',4,'Waste that raises the temperature of a lake can reduce the oxygen available to fish. Temperature is an example of an:','["predator", "biotic factor", "food chain", "abiotic factor"]'::jsonb,3,'Temperature is nonliving.','original'),
('127.462.d.6.C',3,'Biotech companies analyze the leftover materials from production, such as spent fermentation broth, to decide how to safely handle the:','["refuse", "mascot", "sales", "hiring"]'::jsonb,0,'Refuse analysis guides disposal.','original'),
('127.462.d.6.C',4,'Testing manufacturing waste for toxins before release helps a company to follow:','["game rules", "environmental regulations", "fashion trends", "lunch menus"]'::jsonb,1,'Testing ensures compliance.','original'),
('127.462.d.6.D',3,'Too much waste can harm a person''s health, a community''s water supply, and the broader:','["playground", "lunchroom", "environment", "hallway"]'::jsonb,2,'Waste affects many levels.','original'),
('127.462.d.6.D',4,'A factory''s waste in a river can harm the individual who drinks the water, the community that relies on it, and the:','["weekend", "wardrobe", "menu", "ecosystem"]'::jsonb,3,'Impacts extend from one person to nature.','original'),
('127.462.d.6.E',3,'Bacteria that eat oil spilled in soil are used to clean it up. This solution uses living organisms and is called:','["bioremediation", "bioluminescence", "biofuel", "bioethics"]'::jsonb,0,'Microbes clean pollution.','original'),
('127.462.d.6.E',4,'A company using plants to remove metals from contaminated soil is also an example of a:','["sports drill", "bioremediation solution", "marketing plan", "tax credit"]'::jsonb,1,'Phytoremediation is one type.','original'),
('127.462.d.6.F',3,'Laws that limit what companies can release into air and water support waste management by requiring:','["less testing", "more waste", "permits and monitoring", "no records"]'::jsonb,2,'Regulations enforce management.','original'),
('127.462.d.6.F',4,'Evidence that recycling and treatment lower pollution is used to support waste management:','["menus", "hobbies", "games", "policies"]'::jsonb,3,'Policies rely on evidence.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.A',3,'Developing crops that resist insects through selective breeding or gene transfer is an application of:','["agricultural biotechnology", "medical biotechnology", "marine biology only", "space technology"]'::jsonb,0,'Ag biotech improves crops.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.A',4,'Drought-resistant corn is an example of a crop improved through:','["road building", "biotechnology", "clothing design", "weather control"]'::jsonb,1,'Traits are enhanced.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.B',3,'Making cheese, yogurt, and bread with microbes is an example of fermented food production in:','["law enforcement", "sports medicine", "industrial biotechnology", "architecture"]'::jsonb,2,'Fermentation makes foods.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.B',4,'Enzymes added to laundry detergent to break down stains are an example of an industrial:','["sports drink", "road sign", "mineral", "biotechnology product"]'::jsonb,3,'Industrial enzymes are used in many products.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.C',3,'Vaccines, antibiotics, and insulin are examples of products from medical and:','["pharmaceutical biotechnology", "film editing", "road building", "fashion design"]'::jsonb,0,'Drugs result from biotech.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.C',4,'Monoclonal antibodies used to treat some cancers are made using biotechnology, so they are examples of:','["toys", "medical applications", "farm tools", "clothing"]'::jsonb,1,'Modern therapies use biotech.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.D',3,'Sequencing the DNA of a virus to understand how it spreads is an example of:','["accounting", "landscaping", "research and development", "marketing"]'::jsonb,2,'Research drives innovation.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.D',4,'A research team that develops a new test to detect disease from a drop of blood is performing biotechnology:','["retail", "sales", "shipping", "research and development"]'::jsonb,3,'R and D creates new tools.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.E',3,'Analyzing DNA from a crime scene to match a suspect is an application of biotechnology in:','["forensics", "fashion", "cooking", "music"]'::jsonb,0,'DNA profiling aids investigation.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.E',4,'Using genetic tests to identify a species of fish being sold in a market is an application in:','["architecture", "food and environmental science", "sports", "fashion design"]'::jsonb,1,'DNA barcoding confirms species.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.F',3,'Before a genetically modified crop can be sold, companies must follow ethical considerations, laws, and:','["rumors", "wishes", "regulations", "guesses"]'::jsonb,2,'Laws govern release.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.F',4,'Labeling rules for foods made with biotechnology are examples of:','["suggestions", "jokes", "advertisements", "regulations"]'::jsonb,3,'Labels follow rules.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.G',3,'A compound light microscope is used in a biotechnology lab to magnify:','["cells and microorganisms", "vehicles", "mountains", "buildings"]'::jsonb,0,'Microscopes show tiny things.','original'),
('127.462.d.7.G',4,'A centrifuge spins samples at high speed to separate their parts by:','["weight of the tube", "density", "cost", "color"]'::jsonb,1,'Centrifugation separates mixtures.','original'),
('127.472.d.1.A',3,'Ancient Greek medicine, led by Hippocrates around 440 BC, started to explain illness through natural causes rather than only through:','["math", "music", "gods or spirits", "maps"]'::jsonb,2,'Hippocrates shifted thinking.','original'),
('127.472.d.1.A',4,'The belief that illness came from imbalances of four fluids, called humors, was a major idea in medicine for roughly:','["two weeks", "two minutes", "two days", "two thousand years"]'::jsonb,3,'Humoral theory lasted for centuries.','original'),
('127.472.d.1.B',3,'In earlier centuries, illness was often blamed on curses or angry spirits. These are called:','["supernatural explanations", "economic explanations", "scientific explanations", "legal explanations"]'::jsonb,0,'Supernatural beliefs once guided care.','original'),
('127.472.d.1.B',4,'Healers who believed illness had spiritual causes might use prayers, charms, and:','["microscopes", "herbs", "vaccines", "antibiotics"]'::jsonb,1,'Early treatments used plants and rituals.','original'),
('127.472.d.1.C',3,'During the Enlightenment, anatomists and scientists used observation and experiments to learn how the body works. This approach is called the:','["tradition only", "rumor", "scientific method", "lucky guess"]'::jsonb,2,'Enlightenment valued reason.','original'),
('127.472.d.1.C',4,'The invention of the microscope in the 1600s let scientists see tiny organisms for the first time, a key advance of the:','["Stone Age", "Space Age", "Bronze Age", "Enlightenment era"]'::jsonb,3,'Instruments aided discovery.','original'),
('127.472.d.1.D',3,'A common medical practice of the 1700s and 1800s that removed blood from patients to try to cure illness was:','["bloodletting", "vaccination", "imaging", "dialysis"]'::jsonb,0,'Bloodletting was widespread.','original'),
('127.472.d.1.D',4,'Edward Jenner''s smallpox vaccine in 1796 started the field of:','["surgery by saw", "vaccination", "bloodletting", "herbal tea"]'::jsonb,1,'Vaccines began with Jenner.','original'),
('127.472.d.1.E',3,'The introduction of penicillin in the 1940s changed medicine in the United States because it could treat many:','["vision problems", "hearing problems", "bacterial infections", "broken bones"]'::jsonb,2,'Antibiotics saved lives.','original'),
('127.472.d.1.E',4,'The Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act of 1938 required drugs to be proved safe before they could be:','["made at home only", "grown on farms", "advertised for free", "sold to the public"]'::jsonb,3,'Safety rules followed tragedies.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.A',3,'A technician must follow state and federal laws. In Texas, the agency that licenses pharmacists and regulates their practice is the Texas State Board of:','["Pharmacy", "Music", "Tourism", "Sports"]'::jsonb,0,'The board enforces pharmacy law.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.A',4,'The Controlled Substances Act controls drugs that can be abused, such as opioids, by classifying them into:','["menus", "schedules", "brands", "chapters"]'::jsonb,1,'Drugs are scheduled by abuse risk.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.B',3,'A pharmacist fills a prescription with the wrong drug and a patient is harmed. This could lead to a claim of medical:','["generosity", "charity", "malpractice", "teamwork"]'::jsonb,2,'Malpractice involves harm from errors.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.B',4,'A mistake in giving a drug that was caused by a failure to use reasonable care is called:','["kindness", "luck", "accuracy", "negligence"]'::jsonb,3,'Negligence is failure to use care.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.C',3,'A drug maker is sued because a defective medicine harmed users. This kind of claim is called:','["product liability", "copyright", "advertising", "patient privacy"]'::jsonb,0,'Product liability covers defects.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.C',4,'If a patient does not follow directions and is also harmed, the law may consider the patient partly at fault, which is called:','["malpractice", "contributory negligence", "slander", "assault"]'::jsonb,1,'Fault can be shared.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.D',3,'A pharmacist may counsel patients and verify prescriptions, but a technician is not allowed to give:','["counts", "labels", "professional judgment or advice about drug therapy", "stock"]'::jsonb,2,'Roles differ by licensure.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.D',4,'A technician counts pills and prepares a label, and the pharmacist then performs the final:','["cleaning", "delivery", "advertising", "check of the prescription"]'::jsonb,3,'Pharmacists verify.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.E',3,'Pharmacists help manage opioid therapy by checking prescriptions, watching for misuse, and educating patients on safe:','["storage and disposal", "advertising", "parking", "painting"]'::jsonb,0,'Safe handling reduces misuse.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.E',4,'A pharmacist who notices a patient getting several opioid prescriptions from different doctors may contact the prescribers to address a risk of:','["slow service", "overdose or misuse", "low sales", "bad weather"]'::jsonb,1,'Monitoring protects patients.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.F',3,'A technician must never share a customer''s prescription information with someone who is not authorized, because patient information is:','["free", "public", "confidential", "advertised"]'::jsonb,2,'Confidentiality is essential.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.F',4,'Leaving a customer''s printed prescription records on an open counter violates the duty to maintain:','["sales", "inventory", "schedules", "privacy"]'::jsonb,3,'Records must be secured.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.G',3,'A wrongful act that causes harm to another person, which can be the basis of a civil lawsuit, is called a:','["tort", "vote", "budget", "ballot"]'::jsonb,0,'Torts are civil wrongs.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.G',4,'HIPAA is related to medical negligence because releasing records without permission can be a violation that harms the patient''s:','["parking", "privacy rights", "schedule", "meal plan"]'::jsonb,1,'HIPAA protects privacy.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.H',3,'Professional liability insurance protects a pharmacist or technician if a patient claims a mistake caused:','["profit", "growth", "injury", "joy"]'::jsonb,2,'Liability covers claims of harm.','original'),
('127.472.d.3.A',3,'A pharmacy technician at work keeps hair tied back, wears a clean lab coat, and uses closed-toe shoes. This is appropriate:','["costume", "beachwear", "party wear", "professional attire"]'::jsonb,3,'Attire protects safety and image.','original'),
('127.472.d.3.A',4,'Clean, neat uniforms in a pharmacy help prevent contamination of medicines and build patient:','["confidence", "anger", "boredom", "fear"]'::jsonb,0,'Appearance builds trust.','original'),
('127.472.d.3.B',3,'Washing hands before handling medicines is an example of expected personal:','["finances", "hygiene", "hobbies", "style"]'::jsonb,1,'Hygiene prevents contamination.','original'),
('127.472.d.3.B',4,'A pharmacy employee with a cold should stay home or wear a mask because germs could contaminate the:','["wall paint", "parking lot", "medications", "lobby music"]'::jsonb,2,'Illness risks contamination.','original'),
('127.472.d.3.C',3,'A pharmacy technician who is polite, dependable, and honest shows professional:','["rudeness", "avoidance", "laziness", "attitudes and behaviors"]'::jsonb,3,'Professionalism is expected.','original'),
('127.472.d.3.C',4,'Arriving on time, staying focused, and respecting patients are ways to show:','["professionalism", "tardiness", "boredom", "distraction"]'::jsonb,0,'These behaviors build trust.','original'),
('127.472.d.3.D',3,'Speaking clearly, listening, and checking understanding are characteristics of effective communication, while mumbling and ignoring questions are characteristics of:','["accuracy", "ineffective communication", "teamwork", "leadership"]'::jsonb,1,'Behavior affects communication.','original')
