('127.472.d.8.G',3,'Diet, exercise, sleep, and smoking are external factors that affect a person''s:','["birthday", "health", "hair color", "address"]'::jsonb,1,'Lifestyle shapes health.','original'),
('127.472.d.8.G',4,'A patient with high blood pressure who exercises and eats less salt may need a lower dose of medication because lifestyle factors can improve:','["parking", "spelling", "drug response and health", "mood only"]'::jsonb,2,'Lifestyle interacts with therapy.','original'),
('127.472.d.8.H',3,'A pharmacist who reviews all of a patient''s medicines to make sure they work well together and are used correctly is providing:','["customer parking", "inventory counts", "store marketing", "medication therapy management"]'::jsonb,3,'MTM improves therapy outcomes.','original'),
('127.472.d.8.H',4,'Medication therapy management can reduce hospital visits by finding problems such as duplicate drugs and missed:','["doses", "colors", "sizes", "logos"]'::jsonb,0,'MTM improves adherence and safety.','original'),
('127.472.d.9.A',3,'The agency that sets and enforces workplace safety rules in the United States is the:','["Food and Drug Administration", "Occupational Safety and Health Administration", "Postal Service", "Department of Education"]'::jsonb,1,'OSHA protects workers.','original'),
('127.472.d.9.A',4,'OSHA requires employers to train workers about the hazards of chemicals and to provide:','["parking", "bonuses", "protective equipment", "vacations"]'::jsonb,2,'Employers must protect workers.','original'),
('127.472.d.9.B',3,'NIOSH recommends safe handling practices for hazardous drugs because exposure can harm workers'' health. NIOSH stands for the National Institute for Occupational:','["Study and Housing", "Safety and Hunting", "Sales and Hiring", "Safety and Health"]'::jsonb,3,'NIOSH researches worker safety.','original'),
('127.472.d.9.B',4,'NIOSH maintains a list of hazardous drugs that need special handling because they can cause:','["harm to workers", "cheaper bills", "faster service", "higher sales"]'::jsonb,0,'The list guides precautions.','original'),
('127.472.d.9.C',3,'USP chapters set standards for compounding sterile preparations in order to prevent:','["slow service", "contamination of medicines", "low sales", "lost keys"]'::jsonb,1,'USP rules protect patients.','original'),
('127.472.d.9.C',4,'The abbreviation USP stands for United States:','["Pharmacy", "Postal Service", "Pharmacopeia", "Professionals"]'::jsonb,2,'USP sets quality standards.','original'),
('127.472.d.9.D',3,'A chemotherapy drug is a hazardous medication, so a technician handling it should wear special gloves and a gown and follow rules for safe:','["staging", "advertising", "pricing", "disposal"]'::jsonb,3,'Hazardous drugs need protection.','original'),
('127.472.d.9.D',4,'Cleaning up a spill of a hazardous drug should be done with a spill kit, while wearing proper:','["protective equipment", "jewelry", "perfume", "headphones"]'::jsonb,0,'Spills require PPE.','original'),
('127.472.d.9.E',3,'Bloodborne pathogens such as hepatitis B and HIV can be spread through contact with infected blood. Workers prevent exposure by using standard:','["habits", "precautions", "discounts", "fashions"]'::jsonb,1,'Precautions reduce transmission.','original'),
('127.472.d.9.E',4,'A technician who is stuck by a used needle should wash the area and immediately report it to a:','["customer", "neighbor", "supervisor", "friend"]'::jsonb,2,'Prompt reporting enables treatment.','original'),
('127.472.d.9.F',3,'The Hazard Communication Standard requires labels on hazardous chemicals and access to safety data sheets, which help workers know a chemical''s:','["colors", "prices", "brands", "hazards"]'::jsonb,3,'HazCom informs workers.','original'),
('127.472.d.9.F',4,'A safety data sheet for a cleaning chemical tells workers how to use it safely and what to do in an:','["emergency", "audit", "election", "advertisement"]'::jsonb,0,'SDSs give emergency steps.','original'),
('127.472.d.2.H',4,'Malpractice insurance is one form of professional liability coverage, which exists because health workers can be held legally responsible for:','["the weather", "errors that injure patients", "building repairs", "holiday schedules"]'::jsonb,1,'Liability means legal responsibility for harm.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.A',3,'A nurse uses a ratio to figure how much medicine to give a patient from a larger supply. This is applying mathematics to a:','["sports score", "map reading", "health science problem", "music lesson"]'::jsonb,2,'Math supports patient care.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.A',4,'A dietitian calculates calories from grams of fat, protein, and carbohydrates. This is using math in a:','["fashion shop", "film studio", "bank vault", "health science profession"]'::jsonb,3,'Math is used across professions.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.B',3,'A problem-solving model starts by reading the problem and listing what is given and what is:','["asked", "ignored", "hidden", "guessed"]'::jsonb,0,'Analyze givens first.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.B',4,'After solving a medication problem, a good problem solver checks whether the answer is reasonable by:','["guessing again", "evaluating the solution", "skipping the check", "doubling the answer"]'::jsonb,1,'Checking prevents errors.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.C',3,'A student uses a calculator and a ruler to solve a measurement problem. Calculators and rulers are examples of:','["units", "answers", "tools", "problems"]'::jsonb,2,'Tools help solve problems.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.C',4,'Choosing between mental math, paper and pencil, and a calculator depends on the size and difficulty of the:','["classroom", "lunch", "teacher", "calculation"]'::jsonb,3,'Select the right tool for the task.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.D',3,'A student explains her steps for a dosage calculation to a classmate. She is communicating her:','["mathematical reasoning", "hobbies", "opinions", "grades"]'::jsonb,0,'Explaining shows understanding.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.D',4,'When a technician writes out each step of a calculation and labels the units, others can see the logic and catch:','["games", "errors", "jokes", "songs"]'::jsonb,1,'Written steps are checkable.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.E',3,'A nurse makes a table of a patient''s pulse readings across the day. The table helps record and organize:','["recipes", "prizes", "data", "rumors"]'::jsonb,2,'Tables organize information.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.E',4,'Drawing a bar graph of the number of flu cases each month represents data in a form that makes it easy to see:','["hospital colors", "the weather", "staff names", "changes over time"]'::jsonb,3,'Graphs communicate patterns.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.F',3,'A technician sees that doubling a medicine dose doubles the amount of liquid needed. She has found a mathematical:','["relationship", "rumor", "coincidence", "mistake"]'::jsonb,0,'Proportional relationships connect quantities.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.F',4,'Connecting a graph of heart rate to a table of values shows how two ways of representing data are:','["unrelated", "related", "equal in size", "hidden"]'::jsonb,1,'Representations connect.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.G',3,'A student justifies a medication answer by showing the formula, the units, and each step in the calculation. This uses:','["guesswork", "slang", "precise mathematical language", "opinions"]'::jsonb,2,'Justification needs clear language.','original'),
('127.474.d.1.G',4,'To argue that a dosage plan is correct, a health worker could show how the answer follows from the:','["time of day", "lunch menu", "room color", "given information"]'::jsonb,3,'Arguments rest on facts.','original'),
('127.474.d.2.A',3,'A dose is 3/4 of a tablet and the patient takes 2 doses. The total in tablets is:','["1 1/2", "2", "3/4", "2 1/4"]'::jsonb,0,'3/4 x 2 = 1 1/2.','original'),
('127.474.d.2.A',4,'A solution holds 0.25 L. After using 0.075 L, the amount left is:','["0.325 L", "0.175 L", "0.075 L", "0.75 L"]'::jsonb,1,'0.25 - 0.075 = 0.175.','original'),
('127.474.d.2.B',3,'Under First In, Last Out inventory control, the supplies stocked most recently are used:','["only on weekends", "never", "before older supplies", "after older supplies"]'::jsonb,2,'FILO pulls the newest stock first.','original'),
('127.474.d.2.B',4,'Medical supplies with expiration dates are often managed so the earliest-expiring items are used first, which prevents:','["safety", "growth", "savings", "waste"]'::jsonb,3,'Rotation reduces expired stock.','original'),
('127.474.d.2.C',3,'A nurse sees 18 patients in 6 hours. The rate in patients per hour is:','["3", "6", "108", "12"]'::jsonb,0,'18 / 6 = 3.','original'),
('127.474.d.2.C',4,'A hospital staffs 2 nurses for every 8 patients. For 40 patients the number of nurses needed is:','["16", "10", "8", "5"]'::jsonb,1,'40 / 8 x 2 = 10.','original'),
('127.474.d.2.D',3,'In military time, 2:30 PM is written as:','["0230", "1230", "1430", "2030"]'::jsonb,2,'Add 12 hours for afternoon.','original'),
('127.474.d.2.D',4,'A patient is given a medicine at 2100 and the next dose is due 8 hours later. The next dose time in military time is:','["2900", "0900", "0700", "0500"]'::jsonb,3,'2100 + 8 hours = 0500 next day.','original'),
('127.474.d.2.E',3,'A nurse estimates that 48 pills split among 5 bottles is about 10 per bottle. She is using:','["estimation", "measuring", "exact counting", "guessing wildly"]'::jsonb,0,'Estimation checks reasonableness.','original'),
('127.474.d.2.E',4,'Rounding 19.8 mL to the nearest whole number for a quick check gives:','["18 mL", "20 mL", "19 mL", "21 mL"]'::jsonb,1,'Round to nearest whole.','original'),
('127.474.d.2.F',3,'The Roman numeral IV in a health record stands for:','["6", "14", "4", "9"]'::jsonb,2,'IV = 4.','original'),
('127.474.d.2.F',4,'The Roman numeral XIV equals:','["16", "24", "15", "14"]'::jsonb,3,'X + IV = 14.','original'),
('127.474.d.3.A',3,'A scatterplot of hours exercised and resting heart rate lets a student see whether the two variables are:','["related", "identical", "blue", "random labels"]'::jsonb,0,'Scatterplots show relationships.','original'),
('127.474.d.3.A',4,'Fitting a line to a scatterplot of dose and response is using a function to model the:','["rainfall", "trend in the data", "school", "calendar"]'::jsonb,1,'Models fit data.','original'),
('127.474.d.3.B',3,'A medicine''s amount in the blood after each hour is half the amount from the hour before. This pattern is modeled by an:','["constant", "absolute value", "exponential function", "linear function"]'::jsonb,2,'Halving repeatedly is exponential decay.','original'),
('127.474.d.3.B',4,'A function with a constant rate of change, such as an IV that drips 30 mL each hour, is called:','["logarithmic", "periodic", "exponential", "linear"]'::jsonb,3,'Constant change is linear.','original'),
('127.474.d.3.C',3,'A patient''s breathing rises and falls in a repeating pattern. Repeating patterns like this are modeled using a:','["sinusoidal function", "absolute value", "linear equation", "constant"]'::jsonb,0,'Waves are sinusoidal.','original'),
('127.474.d.3.C',4,'The time it takes for one full breathing cycle on a sinusoidal graph is called the:','["range", "period", "slope", "intercept"]'::jsonb,1,'Period is the length of one cycle.','original'),
('127.474.d.3.D',3,'Two medicines are mixed to form a solution of a desired strength. Finding the amounts of each uses a system of:','["posters", "recipes", "equations", "charts only"]'::jsonb,2,'Mixtures lead to systems.','original'),
('127.474.d.3.D',4,'If x + y = 10 and x - y = 4, then x is:','["14", "3", "6", "7"]'::jsonb,3,'Adding gives 2x = 14, so x = 7.','original'),
('127.474.d.3.E',3,'The growth of bacteria in a lab dish that doubles every hour can be solved using properties of:','["exponential functions", "proportions only", "linear functions", "right triangles"]'::jsonb,0,'Doubling is exponential.','original'),
('127.474.d.3.E',4,'The inverse of an exponential function, used to solve for time in a growth problem, is the:','["reciprocal", "logarithm", "square root", "absolute value"]'::jsonb,1,'Logs undo exponentials.','original'),
('127.474.d.3.F',3,'A unit rate compares a quantity to one unit. If an IV delivers 120 mL in 4 hours, the unit rate is:','["120 hours per mL", "480 mL per hour", "30 mL per hour", "4 mL per hour"]'::jsonb,2,'120 / 4 = 30.','original'),
('127.474.d.3.F',4,'A pharmacy fills 90 prescriptions in 5 hours. The unit rate is:','["450 per hour", "85 per hour", "5 per hour", "18 prescriptions per hour"]'::jsonb,3,'90 / 5 = 18.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Career Development' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
insert into public.question_bank (teks_code, teks_item_id, subject, grade_num, course, difficulty, qtype, prompt, choices, answer_idx, explanation, source, status, gen_batch)
select distinct on (v.prompt) v.code, i.id, d.subject,
       case when i.grade_level = 'K' then 0 when i.grade_level ~ '^[0-9]+' then substring(i.grade_level from '^[0-9]+')::smallint end,
       case when i.grade_level is null then i.grade_course_title end,
       v.diff, 'mc', v.prompt, v.choices, v.ans, v.expl, v.src, 'checked', 'cte_69_v1'
from (values
('127.474.d.4.A',3,'A dietitian, a nurse, and a radiologic technologist each use math in different ways. Each profession requires a unique set of:','["skills", "colors", "games", "songs"]'::jsonb,0,'Professions differ in math use.','original'),
('127.474.d.4.A',4,'Dental hygienists measure pocket depths in millimeters, while pharmacists calculate doses in milligrams. Both use math, but with different:','["names only", "units and tools", "holidays", "seasons"]'::jsonb,1,'Fields use different units.','original'),
('127.474.d.4.B',3,'Measuring a patient''s height wrongly could change a medicine dose based on body size, so accurate measurements are necessary to protect:','["menus", "parking", "patient safety", "staff breaks"]'::jsonb,2,'Accuracy prevents harm.','original'),
('127.474.d.4.B',4,'A pharmacy scale off by 5 percent could give patients too much or too little of a drug. This shows why measurements must be:','["loud", "fast", "colorful", "accurate"]'::jsonb,3,'Accuracy matters.','original'),
('127.474.d.4.C',3,'To change 2.5 kg to grams, a technician multiplies by 1,000 using the method of dimensional analysis, which keeps track of the:','["units", "colors", "dates", "names"]'::jsonb,0,'Units cancel in dimensional analysis.','original'),
('127.474.d.4.C',4,'Using dimensional analysis, an IV running at 30 mL per minute, multiplied by 60 minutes per hour, delivers:','["90 mL per hour", "1,800 mL per hour", "180 mL per hour", "0.5 mL per hour"]'::jsonb,1,'30 x 60 = 1,800 and the minutes cancel.','original'),
('127.474.d.4.D',3,'Teaspoons and tablespoons belong to the household system, while milliliters belong to the:','["nautical system", "apothecary system", "metric system", "Roman system"]'::jsonb,2,'Classify units by system.','original'),
('127.474.d.4.D',4,'Grains and drams are old units that belong to the:','["metric system", "decimal system", "SI system", "apothecary system"]'::jsonb,3,'Apothecary units are old.','original'),
('127.474.d.4.E',3,'To measure the temperature of a patient, a nurse selects a thermometer. To measure weight, she selects a:','["scale", "stopwatch", "ruler", "protractor"]'::jsonb,0,'Right tool for the quantity.','original'),
('127.474.d.4.E',4,'A goniometer measures the angle of a joint. Choosing it for this job is selecting an appropriate:','["recipe", "measurement tool", "bill", "diagnosis"]'::jsonb,1,'Tools match tasks.','original'),
('127.474.d.4.F',3,'A nurse takes a pulse by counting beats for 15 seconds and multiplying by 4. She is using a measurement:','["accident", "color", "technique", "rumor"]'::jsonb,2,'Techniques give measurements.','original'),
('127.474.d.4.F',4,'To find an accurate blood pressure, a nurse places the cuff at heart level and rests the patient first, using proper measurement:','["tricks", "rumors", "guesses", "procedures"]'::jsonb,3,'Proper technique improves accuracy.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.A',3,'A lab report shows a patient''s blood sugar is above the normal range. A health worker compares the value to the reference range to see if it lies:','["outside normal limits", "inside the lab", "near the front", "on the report"]'::jsonb,0,'Reference ranges guide interpretation.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.A',4,'A hemoglobin result below the reference range is flagged because it may indicate:','["a normal result", "a health problem", "good health", "a printing error only"]'::jsonb,1,'Values outside limits need attention.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.B',3,'A resting adult heart rate of 140 beats per minute is above the typical range of about 60 to 100. A nurse comparing this to normal parameters would note it as:','["slightly low", "normal", "abnormally high", "perfect"]'::jsonb,2,'Vital signs have ranges.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.B',4,'A temperature of 98.6 degrees Fahrenheit is considered:','["a fever", "hypothermia", "shock", "normal body temperature"]'::jsonb,3,'98.6 is the usual average.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.C',3,'The average of 70, 72, and 74 beats per minute is:','["72", "70", "74", "216"]'::jsonb,0,'(70 + 72 + 74)/3 = 72.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.C',4,'The median of the blood glucose values 90, 100, 95, 120, and 105 is:','["102", "100", "105", "95"]'::jsonb,1,'Ordered 90, 95, 100, 105, 120: middle is 100.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.D',3,'Many biological measurements such as height follow a bell-shaped curve called the:','["straight line", "spiral", "normal distribution", "square wave"]'::jsonb,2,'Normal curves are symmetric.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.D',4,'In a normal distribution, the mean, median, and mode are all located at the:','["far right", "far left", "bottom", "center"]'::jsonb,3,'They coincide at the peak.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.E',3,'According to the Empirical Rule, about 68 percent of data in a normal distribution fall within how many standard deviations of the mean?','["one", "four", "three", "two"]'::jsonb,0,'68-95-99.7.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.E',4,'If the mean cholesterol is 190 with a standard deviation of 20, about 95 percent of values fall between:','["130 and 250", "150 and 230", "190 and 210", "170 and 210"]'::jsonb,1,'190 plus or minus 40.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.F',3,'A z-score tells how many standard deviations a value is from the mean. A value equal to the mean has a z-score of:','["-1", "100", "0", "1"]'::jsonb,2,'The mean is zero deviations away.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.F',4,'A patient''s score is 80 when the mean is 70 and the standard deviation is 5. The z-score is:','["5", "0.5", "10", "2"]'::jsonb,3,'(80 - 70)/5 = 2.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.G',3,'A child''s height is at the 75th percentile. This means the child is taller than about 75 percent of children of the same:','["age", "name", "state", "weight only"]'::jsonb,0,'Percentiles compare to a group.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.G',4,'If 18 of 20 students scored lower than a student, her percentile rank is:','["10", "90", "20", "18"]'::jsonb,1,'18/20 = 90 percent.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.H',3,'A well-designed clinical experiment uses a control group, random assignment, and a large enough:','["budget only", "logo", "sample", "slogan"]'::jsonb,2,'Good design reduces bias.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.H',4,'Assigning patients to treatment and control groups by chance is called:','["favoritism", "guessing", "sorting by age", "randomization"]'::jsonb,3,'Random assignment reduces bias.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.I',3,'A study surveys 200 patients to learn about all patients in a hospital. The 200 patients are a:','["sample", "census", "parameter", "population"]'::jsonb,0,'A sample is a part of the group.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.I',4,'All of the patients in a hospital make up the:','["sample", "population", "control", "bias"]'::jsonb,1,'The population is the whole group.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.J',3,'In a drug trial, a pill with no active ingredient is a placebo, and the improvement some patients report from it is the placebo:','["dose", "price", "effect", "error"]'::jsonb,2,'Expectation can affect results.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.J',4,'Researchers use a placebo group so that they can tell whether a drug works better than:','["no patients", "the first trial", "a larger dose", "a pill with no active ingredient"]'::jsonb,3,'Control is needed.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.K',3,'The study of how often diseases occur in groups of people and what affects them is called:','["epidemiology", "chemistry", "anatomy", "physiology"]'::jsonb,0,'Epidemiology uses statistics.','original'),
('127.474.d.5.K',4,'An epidemiologist who counts new flu cases each week and compares them across counties is using statistics to help guide:','["fashion trends", "public health decisions", "tax laws", "sports schedules"]'::jsonb,1,'Statistics support public health.','original'),
('127.474.d.6.A',3,'The volume of a rectangular tank 20 cm by 10 cm by 5 cm is:','["35 cubic centimeters", "200 cubic centimeters", "1,000 cubic centimeters", "100 cubic centimeters"]'::jsonb,2,'20 x 10 x 5 = 1,000.','original'),
('127.474.d.6.A',4,'A cylinder-shaped medicine bottle has a radius of 2 cm and a height of 10 cm. Using pi = 3.14, its volume is about:','["62.8 cubic centimeters", "40 cubic centimeters", "31.4 cubic centimeters", "125.6 cubic centimeters"]'::jsonb,3,'3.14 x 4 x 10 = 125.6.','original'),
('127.474.d.6.B',3,'A burn covers a rectangle of skin 10 cm by 6 cm. Its surface area is:','["60 square centimeters", "32 square centimeters", "16 square centimeters", "600 square centimeters"]'::jsonb,0,'10 x 6 = 60.','original'),
('127.474.d.6.B',4,'The surface area of a circular bandage with a radius of 3 cm is about, using pi = 3.14:','["18.8 square centimeters", "28.3 square centimeters", "56.5 square centimeters", "9.4 square centimeters"]'::jsonb,1,'3.14 x 9 = 28.26.','original'),
('127.474.d.6.C',3,'A therapist positions a patient''s arm so that it forms a right angle. The angle measures:','["360 degrees", "45 degrees", "90 degrees", "180 degrees"]'::jsonb,2,'Right angle is 90.','original'),
('127.474.d.6.C',4,'Two angles in a straight line, one of 135 degrees, require a second angle of:','["135 degrees", "90 degrees", "225 degrees", "45 degrees"]'::jsonb,3,'Supplementary angles add to 180.','original'),
('127.474.d.6.D',3,'A goniometer reads a knee bending from 10 to 130 degrees. The range of motion is:','["120 degrees", "240 degrees", "60 degrees", "140 degrees"]'::jsonb,0,'Range = end minus start.','original'),
('127.474.d.6.D',4,'A patient''s elbow begins at 20 degrees and bends to 135 degrees. The range of motion is:','["20 degrees", "115 degrees", "135 degrees", "155 degrees"]'::jsonb,1,'135 - 20 = 115.','original')
